# Caddy site for the PropBot webhook receiver — deployment option A (docs/runbook.md §5).
# Copy to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile and replace the host name with your DuckDNS sub-domain.
#
# Caddy obtains and renews the Let's Encrypt certificate by itself. With port 443 restricted to
# TradingView by the firewall, the certificate is issued through the HTTP challenge, so port 80
# must stay reachable from the internet (Caddy answers the challenge and redirects the rest).
#
# Only POST /tv/webhook from TradingView's four webhook source addresses reaches the bot;
# every other request is dropped without an answer. The address list must be identical to
# webhook.source_ips in config/tv_alerts.yaml (a test compares the two files).
# Caddy overwrites any X-Forwarded-For sent by the client with the real peer address, and the
# bot checks that address again (receiver.trusted_proxies / client_ip_header).

ALT-ALAN-ADIN.duckdns.org {
	@tradingview {
		method POST
		path /tv/webhook
		remote_ip 52.89.214.238 34.212.75.30 54.218.53.128 52.32.178.7
	}

	handle @tradingview {
		request_body {
			max_size 4KiB
		}
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
	}

	handle {
		abort
	}
}
